'Ideal' N to resolve a 0.05 (5-point) attack effect with margin: 98,119
'Ideal' N under a stricter 0.02 (2-point) target: 1,348,645

At the 'ideal' N=98,119, the estimator's spread is already +/-0.0087 -- 5.7x smaller than the effect size itself. Section 5's N=2,000,000 tightens that further to only +/-0.0024, for 20.4x the pulses and roughly 20.4x the compute -- narrowing noise that was already well below the effect sizes this notebook cares about resolving. That is the 'smoothing irrelevant noise' this convergence study set out to check: N=2,000,000 is not WRONG, it just buys much more precision on qber_total alone than qber_total alone needs -- its real justification is Section 2.4's original one, the WINDOWED temporal features (qber_dispersion, spectral_entropy, ...), which need many more sifted bits per window than a single pooled QBER estimate ever did.
